  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 AM EDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis and Forecast:\n\nBy the start of the forecast period this morning, sfc low pressure \ncenter will be somewhere near the Leelanau Peninsula. Aloft, a few \nperturbations within the west to east flow will be situated \nupstream. These associated feature at the sfc and aloft move east \nwith time through the day today. Generally to the north of this low \npressure system (Tip of the Mitt and Eastern Upper) will reside\nthe best potential for additional/lingering showers and a few \nstorms, best chances through the morning and midday hours, \nwaning with time through the afternoon. Moisture does evacuate \naloft, and thus there could be a period of very light showers \nand drizzle in spots given the somewhat saturated 0-1 km level, \nnear/north of M-32, midday to early afternoon. Otherwise, a \ncooler day expected, at least a step in that direction, as \nnorthwest winds envelop northern Michigan behind the low \npressure system. More tranquil heading into tonight, although \nthere may be a small chance for a few light showers aided by \nH500 and H250 jet streaks aloft. Overall though, cooler \ntemperatures expected tonight with decreasing surface winds, and\ndry most areas/most of the time.\n\nCouple of features to monitor during the mid to late week time \nframe. Main feature will be a positively tiled upper trough that \ndives down across the Great Lakes region on Friday, bringing good \nsynoptic lift and rain chances to the region. An additional upper \nlow moves out of the Desert Southwest, weakening as it moves \nnortheast, but the sfc reflection will advect modest moisture \nnortheast, with an initial surge of precipitation and rain chances. \nIn reality, precipitation chances from this event will be driven \nfrom both of these features and their interaction, with the \npotential for a healthy rainfall for northern MI. However, the main \nstory with the late week system will be the strong cold advection on \nFriday coupled with 35 to 45 knots of H850 winds. Consequently, \nwindy conditions, potential for 30 to 40 mph, higher along the Lake \nMichigan shoreline, will be possible (fine tuneage in the coming \ndays). As an additional note, there could be a few showers on \nWednesday, but that will not be the focus of the mid to late week \ntime frame.\n\nHeights rise aggressively after the aforementioned trough with sfc \nhigh pressure building in. This will result in precipitation free \nweather through the remainder of the weekend, and slightly milder by \nSunday. Although fantasy land, ridging looks to build next week as \nwell, which would lead to a period of warm, and potentially dry, \nweather. The CPC outlook echos this sentiment, with high \nprobabilities for above normal temperatures.",
